which confirmed he had consumed a fatal amount of antifreeze. Antifreeze poisoning results in a gradual build-up of symptoms, starting with slurred speech, confusion, poor coordination, nausea and drowsiness. As the substance is metabolised, it breaks down into highly toxic acids that rapidly attack the body, leading to severe metabolic acidosis, dehydration, abdominal and back pain, and increasing neurological decline. The most serious damage typically emerges in the kidneys, where crystal formation can cause acute renal failure, dramatically reducing urine output and triggering further systemic collapse. In its final stages, untreated poisoning can progress to heart rhythm disturbances, seizures,

